### WHZ LoopRoom 🎧
WHZ LoopRoom is a web app that turns any song into clean background music (BGM) directly in the browser.

You upload a track, tweak a few controls, and get a remixed BGM version you can download.

### Live Demo
👉 https://whz-looproom.streamlit.app/

# 🎧 Automated Lofi & Slowed + Reverb Audio Generator

A production-ready Python application and REST API that automatically transforms standard audio tracks into atmospheric **Slowed + Reverb** and **Lofi** remixes in seconds. 

Built using **FastAPI**, **Streamlit**, and **Librosa**—no manual editing in DAWs like FL Studio or Audacity required.

---

## 📋 Prerequisites

Before running the app, ensure you have the following two dependencies installed on your system:

### 1. Python 3.10 or Higher
* Download Python from [python.org/downloads](https://www.python.org/downloads/).
* ⚠️ **IMPORTANT (Windows Users):** During installation, make sure to check the box that says **"Add Python to PATH"** at the bottom of the installer window.

### 2. FFmpeg (Required for Audio Exporting)
FFmpeg processes and encodes `.mp3` and `.wav` audio files.

* **Windows:**
  1. Download the release build from [gyan.dev/ffmpeg/builds/](https://www.gyan.dev/ffmpeg/builds/ffmpeg-release-essentials.zip).
  2. Extract the folder and move it to `C:\ffmpeg`.
  3. Search for **Environment Variables** in Windows Search -> Edit System Variables -> Click **Path** -> Click **Edit** -> Add `C:\ffmpeg\bin`.
* **macOS:** Install via Homebrew: `brew install ffmpeg`
* **Linux (Ubuntu/Debian):** Install via APT: `sudo apt update && sudo apt install ffmpeg`

---

## 🚀 How to Run the App

Choose the method that works best for your operating system and technical background:

### Method 1: One-Click Quick Launch (Windows Only) ⚡
*Best for non-technical users, content creators, and quick playback.*

1. Download or clone this repository to your computer.
2. Double-click the **`start.bat`** file located in the root directory.
3. The script will automatically check Python, install required libraries (`pip install -r requirements.txt`), start the FastAPI backend, and launch the Streamlit Web UI in your default browser.

---

### Method 2: Manual Terminal Setup (Mac, Linux & Windows Fallback) 🛠️
*Best for developers, troubleshooting, or non-Windows operating systems.*

#### Step 1: Clone or Download the Project
```bash
git clone [https://github.com/YOUR_USERNAME/YOUR_REPO_NAME.git](https://github.com/YOUR_USERNAME/YOUR_REPO_NAME.git)
cd YOUR_REPO_NAME
Step 2: Set Up a Virtual Environment (Optional, but Recommended)
Bash
# Windows
python -m venv venv
venv\Scripts\activate

# macOS / Linux
python3 -m venv venv
source venv/bin/activate
Step 3: Install Required Libraries
Bash
pip install -r requirements.txt
Step 4: Start the FastAPI Backend
Open a terminal window and run:

Bash
uvicorn main:app --reload --host 127.0.0.1 --port 8000
The backend API will be running at http://127.0.0.1:8000. You can view interactive Swagger API docs at http://127.0.0.1:8000/docs.

Step 5: Start the Streamlit Web Interface
Open a second terminal window (keep the first one running) and run:

Bash
streamlit run app.py
Your browser will automatically open http://localhost:8501 where you can upload audio and test the sliders.

🛠️ Project Structure
Plaintext
├── main.py              # FastAPI Backend API & DSP algorithms
├── app.py               # Streamlit Interactive Frontend UI
├── start.bat            # 1-Click Windows Auto-Launcher
├── requirements.txt     # Python dependency list
└── README.md            # Documentation & setup guide
🔍 Troubleshooting & Common Issues
'python' is not recognized as an internal or external command

Fix: Python was installed without checking "Add Python to PATH". Uninstall Python and reinstall it, ensuring the PATH option is selected.

FFmpeg not found or Audio Processing Fails

Fix: FFmpeg is missing from your system environment variables. Follow the FFmpeg installation steps above and restart your terminal/PC.

Streamlit fails to connect to the backend

Fix: Ensure the FastAPI backend is running on port 8000 before launching app.py.

